      "text": "To find out how many times Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights to lift the same total weight as the original setup with two 20-pound weights, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the total weight lifted with the original setup:**\n   - Weight of each weight: 20 pounds.\n   - Number of repetitions: 12.\n   - Total weight lifted with one 20-pound weight: \\( 20 \\text{ pounds} \\times 12 \\text{ repetitions} = 240 \\text{ pounds} \\).\n   - For two 20-pound weights, the total weight lifted is: \\( 240 \\text{ pounds} \\times 2 = 480 \\text{ pounds} \\).\n\n2. **Set up the equation for the new setup:**\n   - Number of repetitions with the new weights: Let this be \\( x \\).\n   - Weight of each weight: 15 pounds.\n   - Total weight lifted with one 15-pound weight: \\( 15 \\text{ pounds} \\times x \\text{ repetitions} = 15x \\text{ pounds} \\).\n   - For two 15-pound weights, the total weight lifted is: \\( 15x \\text{ pounds} \\times 2 = 30x \\text{ pounds} \\).\n\n3. **Equate the total weights lifted in both setups:**\n   \\[\n   30x = 480\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve for \\( x \\):**\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{480}{30} = 16\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, Terrell must lift the two 15-pound weights \\(\\boxed{16}\\) times to lift the same total weight.",
